prompts.body:
Write {{count}} distinct ad variants for {{platform}}.

Product and offer: {{offer}}
Audience: {{audience}}
Primary angle to emphasize: {{angle}}
Must include: {{must_include}}
Must avoid (claims, words, topics): {{avoid}}

Constraints
- Respect these limits strictly: {{limits}}. Count characters for every line and show the count in brackets.
- Each variant must test a different angle (for example: pain point, outcome, social proof, price or risk reversal, curiosity). Label the angle.
- Only make claims that follow from the information above. Do not invent numbers, reviews, or deadlines.
- No clickbait, fake urgency, or misleading promises. Plain language over buzzwords.

Output a table with columns: #, Angle, Headline, Description or body, CTA, Character counts. After the table, add three sentences on which two variants to test first and what result would tell me which angle wins.

You are a senior content strategist. Create a content brief for a writer.

Primary keyword: {{keyword}}
Secondary keywords or topics to cover (optional): {{secondary}}
Site and audience: {{audience}}
Content type: {{format}}
Target length: {{length}} words
Our expertise or unique angle: {{angle}}
Competing pages I have already seen (optional): {{competitors}}

Produce the brief with these sections:
1. Search intent: what the searcher wants, their stage in the journey, and what a satisfying page must deliver in the first screen.
2. Working title options (3) and a recommended meta description of at most 155 characters.
3. Angle: how this piece will be better or different than typical results. Base this on the information given; if you do not know what currently ranks, say so rather than guessing.
4. Outline: H2 and H3 headings with one line each about what the section must say, and the question it answers.
5. Must-include facts, examples, data types or visuals. Mark where first-hand experience or an expert quote is needed.
6. Internal link opportunities (descriptions of relevant pages) and suggested anchor text.
7. FAQ section: 4 questions people really ask.
8. Quality checklist: what the editor should verify before publishing, including claims that need a source.

Rules: no keyword stuffing guidance; write for the reader first. Do not invent statistics. Format as clean markdown a writer can paste into a doc.

Write title tags and meta descriptions for the page below.

Page URL or topic: {{page}}
Primary keyword: {{keyword}}
What the page offers the searcher: {{value}}
Brand name: {{brand}}
Search intent: {{intent}}
Tone: {{tone}}

Requirements
- Provide {{count}} options. Each has a title tag of at most 60 characters (include the brand only if it fits naturally, usually at the end) and a meta description of 120-155 characters.
- Put the primary keyword near the start of the title, in natural wording. Do not repeat it in an awkward way.
- Each meta description should state a concrete benefit or detail, match the search intent, and end with a light prompt to click when it fits. No ALL CAPS, no emoji, no hype words, no unverifiable claims like "best" or "#1".
- Each option should use a different angle (for example: how-to, specificity, outcome, time saved, audience-specific). Label the angle.
- Show the exact character count for each title and description.

Output as a table: Angle, Title, Title chars, Description, Description chars. Finish with a one-line recommendation of which option to publish and why. Remind me that search engines may rewrite snippets.

Write a launch announcement for {{product_name}}.

Channel and format: {{channel}}
What is launching: {{what}}
Who it is for: {{audience}}
The problem it solves: {{problem}}
Key features and what each does for the user: {{features}}
Availability, pricing and any launch offer: {{availability}}
Call to action: {{cta}}
Voice: {{voice}}

Structure
1. A headline (give three options) that states what is new and why a reader would care. No "Introducing" unless it is the clearest option.
2. An opening of two or three sentences describing the problem as the reader experiences it, then what we built.
3. A short section on the most important capabilities, each tied to a concrete outcome. Maximum {{max_features}} items.
4. One honest sentence about who it is for and who it is not for yet, if the information supports that.
5. Availability and pricing, stated plainly.
6. A single call to action.

Constraints
- Only use facts I provided. Do not invent customer quotes, numbers, awards or roadmap promises. Use [placeholder] where I should add a quote or metric.
- Avoid: "game-changing", "revolutionary", "excited to announce" (use a specific, human opener instead), and exclamation marks.
- Match the length to the channel: email under 250 words, LinkedIn under 200 words, blog 400-600 words.

Design and write a {{num_emails}}-email nurture sequence.

Business and offer: {{business}}
Who signs up and how (lead magnet, trial, webinar, etc.): {{signup}}
Goal of the sequence: {{goal}}
Reader's main doubts or obstacles: {{obstacles}}
Assets I can reference (guides, case studies, features, demo): {{assets}}
Sender name and voice: {{sender}}

Step 1: Plan. Give a table with: email number, send timing, purpose, key message, and the single action we want. Make sure each email has a different job (for example welcome and quick win, education, objection handling, proof, offer). Do not repeat the same pitch.

Step 2: Write each email with: two subject line options (under 50 characters), a preview text line (under 90 characters), the body, and a one-line CTA. Keep bodies under {{max_words}} words, short paragraphs, conversational.

Rules
- Honest, useful copy. No fake urgency, fake scarcity, or implied personal relationship that does not exist.
- Do not invent testimonials, statistics or features. Use [placeholder] where I need to add them.
- Include an unsubscribe-friendly tone (never guilt the reader) and a note on where the footer and unsubscribe link go.
- Finish with 3 metrics to watch and one change to test first.

Repurpose the source content below into social posts.

Source type: {{source_type}}
Audience: {{audience}}
Main takeaway I want people to remember: {{takeaway}}
Link to include (if any): {{link}}
Voice: {{voice}}
Platforms: {{platforms}}

Source:
"""
{{source}}
"""

For each platform, write 3 different posts, each built on a different hook type (a surprising detail, a practical tip, a short story or question). Follow native norms: LinkedIn posts of 600-1200 characters with a strong first line and line breaks; X posts under 280 characters (and one thread of up to 5 posts); Instagram captions of 150-400 characters with a suggested visual or carousel idea.

Rules
- Every claim must come from the source. Do not add statistics or outcomes that are not in it.
- Write each post to stand alone; do not simply cut the original into pieces.
- No engagement bait ("comment YES"), no more than {{hashtags}} hashtags per post, no emoji walls.
- Label each post with platform and hook type, and put the link only where it suits the platform.

End with a suggested posting order across one week and one sentence on which post to try first.

Act as an on-page SEO reviewer. Audit the page below for the target keyword. Base your findings only on the content I paste; do not claim to know rankings, traffic or what competitors have unless I tell you.

Target keyword: {{keyword}}
Search intent I believe it serves: {{intent}}
Page URL: {{url}}
Current title tag: {{title_tag}}
Current meta description: {{meta}}

Page content (headings marked with #, ##, ###):
"""
{{content}}
"""

Check and report on
1. Intent match: does the page deliver what a searcher for this keyword expects, and early enough?
2. Title and meta: length, keyword placement, appeal. Provide improved versions with character counts.
3. Heading structure: one clear H1, logical H2/H3, and missing subtopics a reader would expect.
4. Content quality: thin sections, vague claims, missing examples, unanswered questions, outdated statements to verify.
5. Keyword use: natural presence in the intro, headings and body, without stuffing. Suggest related terms to add only where they help the reader.
6. Internal links, images and alt text, schema or FAQ opportunities: what to check, since I have not provided markup.
7. Trust signals: author, sources, dates, first-hand experience.

Output: a score out of 10 for each area with a sentence of reasoning, then a fix list ranked High, Medium, Low with the effort for each, then a rewritten intro paragraph (under 100 words).

You are an editor refreshing an older article so it deserves to keep ranking and earning trust.

Article title: {{title}}
Original publish date: {{published}}
Target keyword: {{keyword}}
What changed in the topic or industry since then (what I know): {{changes}}
Audience: {{audience}}
Goal of the refresh: {{goal}}

Original article:
"""
{{article}}
"""

Do the following
1. Diagnose: list what is likely outdated, thin, unclear or off-intent. Flag every statement that could have gone stale (statistics, tool names, prices, laws, dates) as VERIFY, and do not replace them with new figures you cannot confirm.
2. Update plan: sections to keep, rewrite, cut, merge or add, with a reason for each.
3. Write the refreshed article. Preserve what was useful and the existing voice, tighten wording, improve headings and add the missing subtopics. Use [CITE: what source is needed] where a current statistic or source belongs.
4. Provide a new title option, meta description (max 155 characters) and a short "Updated on" note to place under the heading.
5. Give a change log of the edits and a pre-publish verification checklist.

Constraints: do not invent data, quotes or sources. Do not change the URL slug. Keep length within 20 percent of the original unless the plan says otherwise.

Write a product description for an online store.

Product: {{product}}
Key specs and facts (materials, size, features, what is included): {{specs}}
Ideal buyer and how they will use it: {{buyer}}
Main concern buyers have before purchasing: {{concern}}
Primary search phrase to work in naturally: {{keyword}}
Brand voice: {{voice}}
Length: {{length}}

Deliver
1. A product title (under 70 characters) that is clear first and clever second.
2. A one-sentence summary of who it is for and what it does for them.
3. A short description in the requested length: lead with the benefit to the buyer, then support it with concrete details from the specs. Address the main concern directly and honestly.
4. {{bullet_count}} scannable bullets in the form "Detail: why it matters".
5. A meta description under 155 characters.
6. One line of care or usage guidance if the specs allow it.

Rules
- Use only the facts I supplied. If a useful detail is missing (for example dimensions or warranty), list it under "Missing info to add" instead of guessing.
- No superlatives you cannot prove ("best", "perfect", "premium quality") and no filler like "must-have". Prefer sensory or practical specifics.
- Use the primary search phrase once or twice where it reads naturally.

Write subject lines and preview text for an email campaign.

Email type: {{email_type}}
What the email says or offers: {{content}}
Audience and what they already know about us: {{audience}}
Brand voice: {{voice}}
Words or tactics to avoid: {{avoid}}
Number of pairs: {{count}}

Requirements
- Subject lines under 50 characters, preview text under 90 characters. The preview should add information, not repeat the subject.
- Each pair uses a different angle: benefit, specific number or detail, question, curiosity with a real payoff, plain announcement, personalization that is true. Label the angle.
- The subject must be an accurate promise of what is inside. No misleading "Re:" or "Fwd:", no fake urgency, no ALL CAPS, no spam-trigger style punctuation, at most one emoji and only if it suits the voice.
- Mix at least two lowercase or conversational options and two more formal ones.

Output a table: #, Angle, Subject, Subject chars, Preview, Preview chars, What it tests. Then recommend an A/B test pair and say what result would show which approach to use going forward. Mention that I should check final rendering in my email tool.

Build a {{weeks}}-week content calendar for the business below.

Business: {{business}}
Audience: {{audience}}
Business goal for this period: {{goal}}
Channels I can realistically run: {{channels}}
Weekly capacity (hours and who does the work): {{capacity}}
Questions customers actually ask or topics sales and support hear: {{questions}}
Existing content to reuse (optional): {{existing}}

Instructions
1. Start with 3-4 content themes derived from the questions and the goal. Explain in one line how each theme supports the goal.
2. Create the calendar as a table: week, channel, format, working title, audience question it answers, funnel stage (awareness, consideration, decision), primary call to action, estimated hours.
3. Respect my capacity. Do not schedule more hours than I have; if the ideas exceed capacity, show what you cut and why.
4. Reuse: for each flagship piece, list 2 smaller derivatives (for example a post, a short email or a chart) so effort compounds.
5. Define one success metric per piece and one review checkpoint at the halfway mark.

Rules: do not promise rankings or specific traffic. Prefer fewer, better pieces over a full grid. Titles should be specific and concrete, not generic ("5 tips for success").

You are a growth marketer designing experiments. Help me plan A/B tests for the asset below.

Asset: {{asset}}
Current performance (conversion rate, traffic, any known drop-off): {{performance}}
Goal metric: {{metric}}
Audience and traffic source: {{audience}}
Weekly traffic or sends available for testing: {{traffic}}
Known qualitative feedback (surveys, support, session notes): {{feedback}}
Current copy or description of the asset:
"""
{{current}}
"""

Deliver
1. Diagnosis: the 3 most likely reasons performance is weak, each tied to evidence in what I gave you. Say what data I would need to confirm.
2. {{num_tests}} test hypotheses in this format: "Because [observation], we believe changing [element] to [variant] will [effect] for [segment], measured by [metric]." Include the control and variant described concretely, with sample copy.
3. Prioritize with a table scoring Impact, Confidence and Effort from 1 to 5, and an overall rank. Explain the logic in a sentence.
4. For the top test: minimum detectable effect that is realistic for my traffic, rough duration, a guardrail metric, and what I should not change during the test. Tell me plainly if my traffic is too low for a reliable test and suggest an alternative approach (for example, sequential or qualitative testing).

Rules: one change per test. Do not promise lift percentages. Do not invent data.

Write an FAQ section for the page described below.

Page and business: {{page}}
Audience: {{audience}}
Real questions to answer (from customers, sales calls, support or search suggestions): {{questions}}
Facts, policies and numbers you may rely on: {{facts}}
Tone: {{tone}}
Answer length: {{answer_length}}

Instructions
1. Reorder the questions by how often buyers need the answer, and group them under 2-4 short headings if there are more than six.
2. Rewrite each question in the exact, natural way a customer would ask it.
3. Answer directly in the first sentence, then add the detail or condition that matters. Where the honest answer is "it depends", say what it depends on.
4. Use only the facts provided. If a question cannot be answered from them, give a draft answer marked [confirm: what to check] rather than guessing.
5. Suggest 3 additional questions customers are likely to have but did not list.
6. Add a closing line pointing to the best next step (contact, trial or documentation).

Rules: no marketing fluff inside answers, no hidden upsells, no promises beyond the stated policies. Keep each answer self-contained so it can be understood on its own.

Output as markdown with H3 questions and plain-text answers, followed by a note on whether FAQ structured data is appropriate for this page.
